le_path": "PROBLEM_STATEMENT.md"}}\n</tool_call><|im_end|>\n<|im_start|>user\n<tool_response>\n1\tI need a plain-text email draft to IT and department leads that summarizes our top 5 overdue requests with concrete examples pulled from staff notes, not buzzwords.\n2\t\n3\tInputs:\n4\t- input/data/requests.csv\n5\t- input/feedback/ (contains .txt notes; do not hardcode file names—inspect this directory and scan all .txt files directly inside it)\n6\t\n7\tWhat to do:\n8\t1) From input/data/requests.csv, filter to requests where status is either "Open" or "In Progress" AND days_overdue > 0.\n9\t2) Rank the filtered rows by priority (High > Medium > Low), then by days_overdue (descending). If there is still a tie, sort by request_id ascending.\n10\t3) Save the top 5 to output/overdue_top5.csv with exactly these columns and header order: request_id,title,priority,days_overdue,owner.\n11\t4) Build output/email_draft.txt as a clear, concrete email with:\n12\t   - A Subject: line.\n13\t   - A one-sentence opening explaining the purpose.\n14\t   - A bulleted list of 5 lines (one per top item), each containing: request_id, title, priority, days_overdue (as a number with the word "days"), owner, and a verbatim feedback quote that mentions that same request_id. The quote must be wrapped in double quotes and followed by the source file name in parentheses, e.g., (source: sales_feedback.txt).\n15\t   - To extract quotes: scan all .txt files in input/feedback/ in alphabetical file-name order; within each file, use the first line that contains the matching request_id. If no matching line is found in any file, write "No feedback quote found" instead of a quote.\n16\t   - A short closing sentence asking owners to reply with an ETA.\n17\t\n18\tKeep the language concrete and avoid vague promises. Do not invent data—only use what\'s in the CSV and the feedback files.\n19\t\n20\tOutputs (must both be created):\n21\t- output/overdue_top5.csv\n22\t- output/email_draft.txt All file paths are