lat 0.08 surcharge and a 1.5 processing fee on each order regardless of state).\n     - total_refactored must be produced by the refactored logic as described above.\n     - delta = total_refactored - total_old (two decimals).\n\n3) Meeting notes (code review + action items):\n   - Produce output/meeting_notes.md with the following sections (headings in plain text are fine):\n     - Summary: one short paragraph explaining what changed and why (mention the move to South Australia, sourced from README.md).\n     - Findings from Code Review: list at least three concrete issues observed in scripts/process_orders.py (e.g., hardcoded surcharge value, assumptions tied to VIC, config values not used). Reference the constant names you found (e.g., SURCHARGE, PROCESSING_FEE) and indicate their values as they currently appear.\n     - Data Checks: include (a) the states and rates found in data/shipping_rates.json, (b) a count of orders per state from data/orders.csv, and (c) the processing_fee and default_state values before and after your config edit (explicitly show both old and new values).\n     - Refactor Changes: a concise list of the files changed/added and the intent of each change.\n     - Action Items for Next Meeting: at least four specific, testable follow-ups (e.g., add unit tests for surcharge lookup and missing states, confirm whether future surcharges will change periodically and how to update the JSON, consider logging, etc.).\n\nNotes and constraints:\n- Do not introduce external dependencies; stick to the Python standard library.\n- Keep all outputs under the output/ directory or the specified script path.\n- Ensure your refactored script is data-driven by the provided JSON files and the updated config/app.json.\n\nExpected output files and paths:\n- scripts/process_orders_refactored.py\n- config/app.json (modified with default_state = "SA" and use_state_surcharge = true)\n- output/totals_refactored.csv\n- output/totals_comparison.csv\n- output/meeting_notes